1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,807
Good morning everyone.

2
00:00:01,807 --> 00:00:07,470
Today's lecture forms part of the Hospitality and Tourism module.

3
00:00:07,470 --> 00:00:11,570
Last week I looked at the economy end of the hotel business;

4
00:00:11,570 --> 00:00:15,670
this week I'm going to discuss the luxury end of the market.

5
00:00:15,670 --> 00:00:19,160
Let's consider the following scenario...

6
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:25,040
You wake up in the middle of the night in a strange hotel miles

7
00:00:25,040 --> 00:00:28,820
away from home, disoriented most probably from jet lag,

8
00:00:28,820 --> 00:00:33,440
when even the most expensive surroundings can seem empty and dispiriting.

9
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:39,252
You have paid a great deal of money to stay in this first-class hotel

10
00:00:39,252 --> 00:00:40,912
with its contemporary technology,

11
00:00:40,912 --> 00:00:45,479
but according to recent research carried out by an international travel

12
00:00:45,479 --> 00:00:48,800
and public relations company, all is not well.

13
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:55,858
The research suggests that even the most opulent, luxurious hotels seem to have underestimated

14
00:00:55,858 --> 00:01:02,412
the most basic needs of their customers-be they travelling for work or pleasure:

15
00:01:02,412 --> 00:01:09,470
the need to feel at home in surroundings which are both familiar and inviting.

16
00:01:09,470 --> 00:01:16,720
Do these findings, however, apply only to hotels situated in particular areas?

17
00:01:16,720 --> 00:01:21,940
Is it possible that the external environment can affect a guest's well-being?

18
00:01:21,940 --> 00:01:28,456
The company's research covered a whole range of different hotel types, both independent hotels

19
00:01:28,456 --> 00:01:32,180
and those which are part of large chains.

20
00:01:32,180 --> 00:01:39,100
They investigated chic so-called boutique hotels in the heart of downtown business districts, stately

21
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:43,054
mansions located in the depths of beautiful countryside,

22
00:01:43,054 --> 00:01:49,973
and plush hotels built at the edge of tropical beaches surrounded by palm trees

23
00:01:49,973 --> 00:01:51,950
and idyllic blue ocean.

24
00:01:51,950 --> 00:01:59,440
And the research concluded that what was outside the hotel building simply didn't matter.

25
00:01:59,440 --> 00:02:05,640
This is a fascinating revelation and those of you hoping to move into careers in the travel

26
00:02:05,640 --> 00:02:10,745
and leisure industry would be well advised to look at the findings in more

27
00:02:10,745 --> 00:02:11,110
detail.

28
00:02:11,110 --> 00:02:14,440
But back to the main point of this lecture...

29
00:02:14,440 --> 00:02:16,660
the need to feel at home.

30
00:02:16,660 --> 00:02:20,640
What can the hotel industry do about it?

31
00:02:20,640 --> 00:02:26,100
And is the very idea so subjective that it's impossible to do anything about

32
00:02:26,100 --> 00:02:28,050
it on a global basis?

33
00:02:28,050 --> 00:02:32,540
However, nothing stands still in this world.

34
00:02:32,540 --> 00:02:37,699
One company has come up with the slogan 'Take Your Home With You',

35
00:02:37,699 --> 00:02:41,270
and aims to provide clients with luxury serviced apartments.

36
00:02:41,270 --> 00:02:48,730
Those in the business travel industry maintain that these serviced apartments dispense with all the unwanted

37
00:02:48,730 --> 00:02:52,926
and expensive hotel services that business travellers don't want,

38
00:02:52,926 --> 00:02:56,190
while maximising the facilities they do want.

39
00:02:56,190 --> 00:02:59,836
For example, not only sleeping and living accommodation,

40
00:02:59,836 --> 00:03:04,849
but also a sleek modern kitchen that allows guests to cook

41
00:03:04,849 --> 00:03:08,950
and entertain if they wish, at no additional cost.

42
00:03:08,950 --> 00:03:14,410
The attractions of such facilities are obvious and it'll be interesting to see whether

43
00:03:14,410 --> 00:03:18,700
the company manages to establish a trend all over the world

44
00:03:18,700 --> 00:03:22,600
and make a lasting impact on the luxury accommodation market.

45
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:30,730
Now, finally I want to consider the psychology underpinning the traditional holiday hotel industry.

46
00:03:30,730 --> 00:03:38,824
As a hotelier, how do you go about attracting people to give up the security of their own home

47
00:03:38,824 --> 00:03:43,084
and entrust themselves to staying in a completely strange place

48
00:03:43,084 --> 00:03:45,640
and sleeping in an unfamiliar bed?

49
00:03:45,640 --> 00:03:53,560
Firstly, hotels exploit people's need to escape the predictability of their everyday lives.

50
00:03:53,560 --> 00:03:58,922
For a few days people can pretend they are free of responsibilities

51
00:03:58,922 --> 00:04:00,710
and can indulge themselves.

52
00:04:00,710 --> 00:04:05,497
Secondly, there is something very powerful in our need to be pampered

53
00:04:05,497 --> 00:04:10,683
and looked after, it's almost as if we return to being a baby,

54
00:04:10,683 --> 00:04:15,470
when everything was done for us and we felt safe and secure.

55
00:04:15,470 --> 00:04:20,549
And not far removed from this is the pleasure in being spoilt

56
00:04:20,549 --> 00:04:26,475
and given little treats-like the miniscule bottles of shampoo and tiny bars of soap,

57
00:04:26,475 --> 00:04:32,400
the chocolate on your pillow at night-and we actually forget that we are paying

58
00:04:32,400 --> 00:04:33,670
for it all!

59
00:04:33,670 --> 00:04:39,454
Next week, I'm going to look at eco-hotels, a fairly new phenomenon

60
00:04:39,454 --> 00:04:40,900
but increasingly popular...
